<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vicente Fox</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vicente Fox is a Mexican businessman and politician who was president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from 2000 to 2006. A former Coca-Cola executive and governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>, he won the 2000 election for the conservative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-action-party">National Action Party</a>, ending 71 years of rule by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/institutional-revolutionary-party">Institutional Revolutionary Party</a> (PRI). His presidency marked Mexico's peaceful transition to multi-party democracy, though many of his reform plans stalled in Congress.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vicente-fox.jpg" alt="Vicente Fox" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicente_Fox_Official_Photo_2000_(Cropped)_(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
